 "lead": 2,
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Four hundred people danced in the street, for weeks, until the blood ran out of their shoes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And no, buddy, this ain't some bedtime story."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "It's in the history books. They called it the Dancing Plague."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Now listen to how this whole mess started. Strasbourg, the year fifteen eighteen."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "A normal summer day in July. A woman named Frau Troffea walks out of her house, right into the middle of the street, and starts dancing."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "All by herself. No fiddler, no music, not a drop of wine in her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Just hopping around like something bit her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The neighbours looked out and said, well, her husband must have finally run off, and she's celebrating."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Except she didn't dance for five minutes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "She danced day and night, no sleep, barely any food, for almost a whole week."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Her shoes fell apart. Her feet were bleeding. She was crying that she couldn't stop. But her legs just kept on going."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And now comes the really creepy part."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "A few days later, other people started dancing right next to her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "Within a week there were more than thirty. And within a month, some say four hundred folks were jumping around the town square, like a madhouse."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And buddy, they were not dancing because bread got cheaper."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "They were screaming, begging for help. But their arms and legs were moving all on their own, like the devil himself got into them."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "And you know what the doctors and the city council did, to cure them?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "They said the only cure was to let them dance, until the sickness came out of their bones."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "So they built a big wooden stage, and paid musicians with drums and pipes to play non-stop, to keep them in rhythm."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Now how dumb can you be?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "That's like watching your barn burn down, and throwing more wood on it, so the fire finishes faster."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Of course, it was a disaster."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "In the summer heat, starving and worn out, people started dropping like flies."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Chroniclers wrote that some of them died. One even claimed fifteen a day, though historians still argue about that number."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "But why on earth did this happen?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "There are two big theories."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "First: their rye bread might have been spoiled by a poison fungus, called ergot."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "That fungus holds the same stuff they make L S D from today. It gives you spasms and hallucinations, like seeing devils on the walls."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "Second, and most historians like this one: those people were starving, scared to death, and they truly believed Saint Vitus could curse you to dance until you dropped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "So between the bad bread and all that fear, their minds just snapped, and they hopped around for two whole months."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "In the end, they hauled the dancers up to a mountain shrine of Saint Vitus, to pray. And slowly, it stopped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time you complain your feet hurt after two hours at a wedding, think about those poor folks who danced for two months straight."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "Follow for more strange history."
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "July 1518, Strasbourg: Frau Troffea danced in the street for days; about 34 joined within a week; chroniclers claim up to 400 within a month; the council prescribed more dancing (guild halls, a wooden stage, paid musicians); some deaths reported, '15 a day' is one later claim and disputed; dancers taken to a shrine of St Vitus near Saverne; the outbreak faded by September.",
   "working": "July to early September is about two months.",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Dancing plague of 1518; John Waller, A Time to Dance, a Time to Die (2008); Smithsonian; History.com"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Ergot (Claviceps purpurea) on rye produces ergot alkaloids, including lysergic acid, the precursor of LSD; ergotism causes convulsions and hallucinations. Many historians (e.g. Waller) favour mass psychogenic illness driven by famine, disease and belief in St Vitus' curse.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Waller 2008; Britannica, ergotism"
  }
 ],
